 economically liberal very socially  liberal and what David Cameron did was  to make the Tory party more acceptable  to the centre by embracing social  liberalism and what Tony Blair did was  to make the Labour Party more acceptable  to the centre by embracing economic  liberalism and that was all that was  being offered the collapse and Trust in  this elite then gave way to active  revolt  Jeremy Corbyn the election of Jeremy  Corbyn as leader of the Labour Party  signaled a rejection of the ruling  consensus and a decisive shift to the  left Jeremy Corbyn is an economic  radical and he presented the most  radical manifesto in economic terms that  the Labour Party has ever presented I  think then in 2016 the public had their  opportunity to turn in the establishment  over announcement came just after 7 a.m.  [Applause]  [Music]  within three years Boris Johnson one of  the faces of the leave campaign was  leader of a Conservative Party that had  shifted clearly to the right the people  of this country voted to get brexit  Danny  so brexit is part of a much broader  breakdown of the governing political  regime in this country we have at the  moment a Tory party which is moving  towards a sort of populist nationalism  and a Labour Party which is very  left-wing you have to polarize parties  now that creates chaos in all sorts of  ways if Labour were to defy expectations  and take power the party's economic  policies alone would have profound  consequences they have an extremely  ambitious radical set of policies which  is to change entirely the economic model  of this country this country is a very  open economy very dependent on flows of  foreign capital and a lot of that  capital will take flight and a lot of  the policies that they want to put  through will traumatize big corporation  small corporations so we will have an  extremely traumatic period both on  foreign exchanges and domestically  if neither party ends up with the  working majority they're going to find  it harder than ever to form alliances in  Parliament  now they've vacated the center ground if  the Tory party almost wins that doesn't  quite win it won't have anybody to work  with because it's so far to the right  according to a Liberal Democrat or  Scottish nationals that they won't work  with them probably we would have a  coalition of the Labour Party plus the  Scottish Nationalist Party plus the  Liberal Democrats very broad and rather  chaotic coalition such a coalition would  legislate for a second grexit referendum  [Applause]  a solid conservative victory would spare  the country through this fate but don't  expect Forrest Johnson's promise to get  brexit done the smellin ends to chaos  either Horace has promised to do this  extraordinary thing which is getting a  trade agreement with the European Union  by the end of next year that's really  not gonna happen it was too much to do  in time it might lead to a New Deal  breakfast where No Deal breaks it is  extremely disruptive and brexit could  mark the start not the end of the  country's constitutional turmoil  I think we've never been as close to the  breakup of the United Kingdom Boris  Johnson has negotiated a deal with the  European Union which essentially creates  a border down the Irish Sea and that  means that Northern Ireland is separate  in certain very significant ways from  the rest of the United Kingdom and is  being dragged slowly into the orbit of  the Irish Republic so that means that  the unification of Ireland in the long  term is brought nearer for Scotland who  voted to stay in the EU another  independent vote is already on the cards  so the Scots nationalists whenever they  make a speech in Parliament make a  speech about we voted to stay in but  you're taking us out against our will  and so we no longer want to be part of  this this kingdom Scotland has been  totally and utterly shafted that's  boosted support for Scottish  independence because remainders think  that the only way 